# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Compression layer: Sections 8 (Context) and 9 (Contributions)

REVS = {
    # 8.1
    "P542": ("The choice of Greater Tzaneen is not arbitrary,",
             "The choice of Greater Tzaneen is not arbitrary, and it is not a convenience sample in the ordinary sense. The municipality combines the conditions the study needs: a large population with high service connection and low service quality; an agricultural and agro-processing economy with the organic waste streams that follow; a climate warm enough for anaerobic digestion; and a municipal administration under capacity but not absent. It is a place where the contradictions of the South African transition are visible in a small area \u2014 a formal town core surrounded by settlements of varying formality, tenure and services, a few kilometres apart and decades apart in investment. Finally, Limpopo receives less attention in the energy transition literature than Gauteng or the Western Cape despite its population and renewable potential, so a study there adds to a thin evidence base."),
    # 8.2
    "P546": ("Greater Tzaneen Local Municipality (LIM333)",
             "Greater Tzaneen Local Municipality (LIM333), one of four local municipalities in the Mopani District, covers approximately 2,896 square kilometres and had a population of 478,254 at the 2022 census, up from 389,623 in 2011 \u2014 growth of about two per cent a year (Statistics South Africa, 2023). There are roughly 129,600 households, averaging 3.7 people, about half headed by women. Just over 16 per cent of adults have no schooling and under nine per cent a higher education qualification. Table 6.1 sets out the indicators that matter for this study."),
    "P550": ("The contrast between the electricity figure and the water,",
             "The contrast between the electricity figure and the water, sanitation and refuse figures is the point of the table: almost every household is connected for lighting, fewer than a third have water inside the dwelling, and about a quarter have refuse collected. A household here is likely to be grid-connected while managing its own water, waste and cooking fuel \u2014 exactly the configuration the study is about."),
    "P551": ("Two caveats are necessary.",
             "Two caveats are necessary. The dwelling classification counts a structure as formal if it is a house or flat on a formal erf, and backyard rooms or rented shacks on a formal plot may be counted within it, so the 97.1 per cent formal-dwelling figure understates informal or insecure arrangements; the study will not rely on it and will establish tenure through its own scoping. And \u201celectricity for lighting\u201d records use, not the legality, reliability or capacity of the connection: a household with an informal connection from a neighbour's meter is counted as using electricity. The indicator measures presence, not service."),
    # 8.3
    "P554": ("The municipality's energy profile is shaped by three factors.",
             "The municipality's energy profile is shaped by three factors. The national grid: distribution is carried out by Eskom rather than a municipal distributor, so outages, tariffs and connection processes are administered at a distance, and municipal officials can advocate but cannot decide. The local economy: agro-processing generates fruit waste, sawdust, poultry litter, abattoir waste and market waste, some already used for feed or compost, some dumped; a digester would compete for a fraction of this stream, and the competition is part of what the study must understand. Household fuel use: as in much of Limpopo, households combine electricity with wood, paraffin and LPG \u2014 wood often collected rather than bought, paraffin bought in small quantities at a high cost per unit of energy \u2014 and the combination changes with season, price and reliability."),
    # 8.4
    "P560": ("Biogas depends on a feedstock stream and a water supply,",
             "Biogas depends on a feedstock stream and a water supply, both constrained here. Refuse removal reaches about a quarter of households; the rest burn, bury or dump their waste, leaving organic waste dispersed rather than concentrated \u2014 a problem for a digester needing a steady supply. The design response is to aggregate waste from a few sources \u2014 a market, a poultry operation, a group of households \u2014 or to size the digester to the supply that can be guaranteed; the co-design workshops will decide. Water reaches most households through a network, but inside connections are limited and supply intermittent in places; in a settlement where water is carried, a technology needing several hundred litres a week is a different proposition from one needing fifty. The study will measure water use and treat it as a design constraint."),
    # 8.5
    "P564": ("The specific settlement has not been selected.",
             "The specific settlement has not been selected. This is deliberate, a statement about the state of the work rather than an omission. The scoping phase in months one to six will assess candidate settlements against the criteria in Table 5.1 through a site visit, conversations with community structures and a meeting with the municipality, and the outcome will be documented in a site selection memorandum stating which criteria each candidate met and why the chosen site was preferred."),
    "P566": ("What can be said now is what the study expects to find,",
             "What can be said now is what the study expects to find, based on the literature and preliminary conversations: a mix of owner-occupied and rented dwellings, an electricity supply that is connected but unreliable and costly, multiple cooking fuels, uncollected organic waste, water at a shared point. The settlement is likely to have at least one organised structure \u2014 a residents' committee, a women's group, a church-based organisation \u2014 and to have experience of previous projects that shapes how a new one is received. A settlement that has been promised services before will not take a new promise at face value, and the study should not expect it to."),
    # 8.6
    "P568": ("The case is an Engineering Management case,",
             "The case is an Engineering Management case, not only an energy case. The field is concerned with designing, operating and improving systems that combine technical and organisational elements, and a community-scale digester is exactly such a system: a technical core with a supply chain, a maintenance regime, a cost structure, a governance arrangement and users whose practices determine whether it works. The decisions that determine its fate \u2014 siting, sizing, contracting, staffing, pricing, training, scheduling, risk allocation \u2014 are management decisions."),
    "P570": ("The study contributes to the field in three ways:",
             "The study contributes to the field in three ways: a method for specifying the non-technical requirements of a technical system in a low-income context; an evaluation approach that treats justice as a system performance criterion rather than an external constraint; and evidence about the organisational conditions under which a decentralised energy system is sustained. The same questions arise for solar water heating, container-based sanitation, water ATMs and small-scale PV, and the framework is intended to be portable across them."),
    # 8.7
    "P573": ("Access will be negotiated in three steps.",
             "Access will be negotiated in three steps. Institutionally, the study will seek written permission from the municipality and engage the relevant departments \u2014 waste management, electricity, planning, local economic development \u2014 as participants rather than gatekeepers, with a commitment to share findings. At community level, it will approach the recognised structures, explain the purpose and limits of the project, and ask permission to work in the area; it will not offer benefits in exchange for access, and concerns will be recorded and addressed before fieldwork begins. At household level, participation is individual, refusal carries no consequence for service entitlement, and participants may withdraw with data removed on request where practicable. The study will also establish a small advisory group of residents, municipal officials and a technical expert, meeting twice a year: not a decision-making body, but a channel for critique that does not depend on the researcher's judgement."),
    # 8.8
    "P579": ("Greater Tzaneen offers a setting in which nearly every household",
             "Greater Tzaneen offers a setting in which nearly every household is grid-connected while fewer than a third have water inside the dwelling, refuse collection reaches a quarter of households and organic waste is dispersed, and a large share of households are headed by women and depend on multiple cooking fuels. Those conditions make the configuration, rather than the artefact, the central problem. Section 9 states what the study expects to contribute."),
    # 9.1
    "P583": ("A PhD proposal is expected to state its contribution,",
             "A PhD proposal is expected to state its contribution, and there is a temptation to overstate it. This section tries to be precise. The study claims four bounded contributions \u2014 theoretical, methodological, practical and policy-related \u2014 each assessable against the evidence the study will produce. It does not claim a new theory, a solution to energy poverty in South Africa, or a technology that will work everywhere. Table 7.1 sets out the contributions with the evidence supporting each."),
    # 9.2
    "P588": ("The theoretical contribution is the argument that acceptance",
             "The theoretical contribution is the argument that acceptance is an emergent property of a socio-material configuration, with consequences for how energy innovation should be designed and evaluated. The claim is not new in its general form \u2014 practice theory, socio-technical systems theory and actor-network theory have all argued that technology and social order are co-produced. What is new is the specific combination: a posthumanist ontology applied to the design of a community-scale energy system in a South African informal settlement, with the justice literature supplying the normative criteria, yielding propositions that can be examined and, importantly, can fail."),
    "P590": ("The test of the contribution is whether the framework explains",
             "The test of the contribution is whether the framework explains something a TAM study would have missed. The study expects to find at least three such things: households reporting a positive attitude that stop using the system because a material condition changed; households reporting a negative attitude that continue because there is no alternative; and identical technologies producing different outcomes in adjacent households of similar income because the configuration differs in ways an attitude survey cannot detect. If none is found, the contribution is weakened, and the study will report that."),
    # 9.3
    "P593": ("The methodological contribution is the combination",
             "The methodological contribution is the combination of four analytical practices and the documentation of what each contributes. Reflexive thematic analysis is well established but usually used alone; situational mapping is used in grounded theory but rarely in energy research; causal loop diagramming is used in systems research but rarely with qualitative community data; and diffractive reading is used in posthumanist research but rarely in engineering. The study will report how the four were combined, what each produced, and where they produced incompatible accounts. The combination is not offered as a template: it is a documented experiment in method, with an honest account of its costs \u2014 diffractive reading in particular is slow and difficult to explain, and the study will show what it added and what it cost."),
    "P596": ("The second methodological contribution is the operationalisation of justice.",
             "The second methodological contribution is the operationalisation of justice. The energy justice literature is rich in principles and thin in indicators; the twelve indicators in Table 4.4 attempt to close that gap for a project-scale intervention. They are not the only possible set, and the study will report which proved usable."),
    # 9.4
    "P598": ("For practitioners, the study offers a design protocol and a body of evidence.",
             "For practitioners, the study offers a design protocol and a body of evidence. The protocol treats the configuration as the unit of intervention: a project team would specify the feedstock supply, water budget, siting, maintenance arrangement, spare-parts route and governance rules at the same time as the digester volume and gas yield, and the study will produce a template for that specification, derived from and tested against the case. The evidence concerns the conditions under which a community-scale biogas system is sustained: the literature contains many accounts of why systems fail and few longitudinal accounts of how one was maintained, by whom, at what cost. These outputs are modest, but they are the kind a project manager can use; a framework that is elegant but unusable is of limited value in engineering management."),
    # 9.5
    "P603": ("At the policy level, the study speaks to two questions",
             "At the policy level, the study speaks to two questions municipalities currently answer by default. How to evaluate an energy programme against justice criteria: Integrated Development Plans routinely state commitments to redress and inclusion without specifying how they will be assessed, and the indicator set in Table 4.4 offers a way of doing so at project scale with data a municipality could collect. And how to organise participation so that it is more than consultation: the study will document the arrangements that made participation possible for tenants, women and youth, and those that did not \u2014 directly relevant to municipal engagement design and to the conditions attached to Just Energy Transition Partnership funding."),
    # 9.6
    "P608": ("The study is located within a research programme",
             "The study is located within a research programme on gendered energy innovation in poor urban environments led by the supervisor and colleagues, which has produced a gender analysis framework (Anditi et al., 2022), a systems model of energy service security (Batinge et al., 2022), a co-design toolkit (Petrulaityte et al., 2022), a process for developing energy Living Labs (Mukama et al., 2022) and a posthumanist argument for a social approach to energy transition in engineering education (Aboalez, 2025). This study extends that programme by moving the posthumanist argument from engineering education to settlement-level design, adding a justice evaluation the earlier work identified as necessary but did not operationalise, and providing a detailed case in a new site."),
    "P610": ("The relationship is a strength and a risk.",
             "The relationship is a strength and a risk: a build on established concepts and access, but also the danger that a study too close to its supervisor's framework will not test it hard enough. The design addresses this through negative case analysis, a critical friend on the advisory group, and an explicit statement of what would count as disconfirming evidence."),
    # 9.7
    "P612": ("A doctoral study is also a process of formation,",
             "A doctoral study is also a process of formation, and it is reasonable to state what the researcher expects to learn: competence in qualitative research in a context of poverty and inequality; in applying posthumanist theory to engineering problems; and in managing a multi-stakeholder design process. The fieldwork requires working in a language and setting that are not the researcher's own; the theoretical work requires fluency in concepts not native to engineering; and the design work requires the management skills the study is, in part, about."),
    "P614": ("None of this belongs in a claim about contribution to knowledge.",
             "None of this belongs in a claim about contribution to knowledge; it belongs in the statement of what the study is for, and it shapes the choices in Section 10."),
}
